deep.reconstruction:
Setting (Analysis situs, third draft, 1986). Grothendieck works with an axiomatic, purely combinatorial model of a tame space. M is a set of 'pieces' (closed cells or strata) with these structures:
- an order ≤ with meets X∩Y;
- a relation Z ≪ X, read 'Z lies in X';
- for each X, a boundary ∂X and a 'shadow' Omb(X) = {Z : Z ≪ X}, with Omb(X)∩Omb(Y) = Omb(X∩Y);
- a disjointness relation ∥;
- traces Y_{∂X} = Y|∂X;
- an 'open part' X° = {Z ≪ X : Z ∥ ∂X}, written Z ≪̊ X.
The axioms visibly used here are: if Z ≪ ∂X and Z ∥ ∂X then Z ∥ Z, which is absurd; and every proper subpiece L < X satisfies L ⊂ ∂X.

End of the proof begun on p.90. The criterion is: Y ∈ X° iff for all Y' in Y~ (the pieces below Y; the check-accent of p.90), Y'° ⊂ X°. Here he proves ⇐. Assume ⋃_{Y'∈Y~} Y'° ⊂ X°. To get Y ∈ X° it suffices that Y_{∂X} = ∅, i.e. that no Z in Y~ satisfies Z ≪ ∂X. For such a Z the hypothesis gives Z° ⊂ X°. Part a) ('-)') of the criterion then gives Z ∈ X°, so Z ∥ ∂X, contradicting Z ≪ ∂X. Part a) itself needs Z° ≠ ∅, a dependence he flagged on p.90 (nonemptiness via L1 and the divisibility axiom).

NB. For X, Y ∈ M with X ≠ Y, X° ∩ Y° = ∅. A marginal addition strengthens this: X° ∥ Y° (cf. p.95). He first wrote the hypothesis 'X ≱ Y', then added 'X ≠ Y', which is all the proof needs.
Proof: put L = X∩Y and suppose Z ∈ X°∩Y°. Then Z ∈ Omb(X)∩Omb(Y) = Omb(L), so Z ≪ L. Since X ≠ Y, L = X and L = Y cannot both hold; say L ≠ X. Then L ⊂ ∂X, so Z ≪ ∂X. But Z ∥ ∂X because Z ∈ X°, hence Z ∥ Z, absurd.
In modern terms, the open strata of distinct closed pieces are disjoint: the open cells of a face poset partition the space.

Last lines. He starts a new construction: a subset Φ ⊂ M of 'multistrict' pieces, closed under ≤ and hence 'compatible', with the observation Φ = F~. On p.92 this is used to define S_A = Supp(⋃_{X∈A} X°) in the lattice Σ_M of supports. The map P(F~) → Σ_M is monotone and preserves sups: the tame space is reconstructed from its open strata.
